      "affected_components": [
        "https://ticket.ausecurity.best/",
        "https://ticket.ausecurity.best/setup/",
        "https://ticket.ausecurity.best/setup/install.php",
        "http://51.79.166.209:8080/setup/install.php"
      ],
      "references": [
        "https://docs.osticket.com/en/latest/Getting%20Started/Installation.html"
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "status": "finished",
    "data": {
      "title": "DOM-based cross-site scripting on consultation success page",
      "cvss": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:C/C:L/I:L/A:N",
      "summary": "A crafted link on the public website can run an attacker’s script in a visitor’s browser.",
      "description": "consultation-success.html reads the `name` query parameter with URLSearchParams and assigns it to the DOM with innerHTML (element id user-name). The `company` parameter is assigned with textContent and is not this sink. The contact API (POST /api/contact) returns redirect_url=/consultation-success.html?name=<full_name>&company=<company_name>, and main.js sets window.location.href to that value. Direct evidence: the page JavaScript contains `document.getElementById('user-name').innerHTML = userName`. A malicious URL such as /consultation-success.html?name=<img src=x onerror=...> is sufficient proof; the payload was not driven further. Impact is session/script execution in the site origin for any user who opens the link.",
      "recommendation": "Stop using innerHTML for untrusted URL data. Use textContent (as already done for company). Encode output and add a strict Content-Security-Policy. Validate redirect_url on the client to same-origin paths without embedding raw HTML.",
      "affected_components": [
        "https://ausecurity.best/consultation-success.html",
        "https://www.ausecurity.best/consultation-success.html",
        "https://contact.ausecurity.best/consultation-success.html"
      ],
      "references": [
        "https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/DOM_Based_XSS"
      ]
    }
  },
  {
